export const pkUnit01CommonInfoMarkdown: LocalizedText = {
  "en": "# PK Unit 1: Hello and My Class — Common Info\n\n## 1. Unit Theme Overview\n\nPK Unit 1: Hello and My Class helps children move from basic classroom settling into more stable classroom participation. Children revisit greetings, teacher-child interaction, peer awareness, classroom objects, roll call, turn-taking, material handling, and clean-up routines with broader vocabulary and more intentional response.\n\nCompared with PG, PK children can be supported to use short fixed chunks such as `Hello`, `Here`, `OK`, `Yes`, `No`, `Thank you`, and single classroom object words when ready. However, this unit remains a non-language developmental unit. Children are not required to produce full self-introduction sentences, answer full questions, or complete desk-based Starter-style tasks.\n\nThe focus is using classroom life as the context for social participation, self-care, sensory exploration, story and puppet interaction, pretend play, creative expression, music, construction, and PSED/safety routines.\n\n## 2. Unit Language\n\n### PK Core Vocabulary\n\n```text\nhello ｜ bye-bye ｜ goodbye ｜ teacher ｜ friend ｜ classroom ｜ book ｜ bag ｜ chair ｜ table ｜ crayon ｜ pencil ｜ board ｜ window ｜ door ｜ look ｜ listen ｜ sit down ｜ stand up ｜ come here ｜ go back ｜ wait ｜ your turn ｜ point ｜ touch ｜ show me ｜ clean up ｜ here ｜ OK ｜ yes ｜ no ｜ thank you\n```\n\n### Functional Teacher Chunks\n\n```text\nHello, [Name]. ｜ Say hello. ｜ Say goodbye. ｜ Look. ｜ Listen. ｜ Sit down. ｜ Stand up. ｜ Come here. ｜ Go back. ｜ Wait. ｜ It’s your turn. ｜ Point to the book. ｜ Touch the chair. ｜ Show me your bag. ｜ Get your book. ｜ Put it on the table. ｜ Put the crayon here. ｜ Clean up. ｜ [Name], are you here? ｜ Are you here? Yes or no? ｜ Say here. ｜ I’m [Name]. ｜ Good job. ｜ Thank you.\n```\n\n## 3. Weekly Subthemes\n\n### Week 1: Hello, Here and Teacher\n\nChildren strengthen greeting, roll-call, teacher-response, and classroom-entry routines. The focus is responding to name, saying or indicating `Here`, greeting teacher, and moving between teacher and group spaces.\n\n### Week 2: My Classroom Things\n\nChildren revisit and expand classroom object language through books, bags, chairs, tables, crayons, pencils, board, door, and window. The focus is getting, showing, placing, and returning materials.\n\n### Week 3: My Friends and My Turn\n\nChildren practise friend awareness, turn-taking, waiting, passing, giving, taking, and supported short chunks such as `my turn`, `your turn`, `thank you`, and `OK` when ready.\n\n### Week 4: Classroom Routines and Clean-Up\n\nChildren integrate greetings, roll call, classroom objects, turn-taking, and clean-up into more complete classroom routines. The focus is stable participation and increasing independence.\n\n## 4. Teacher Input Pool\n\n### Greeting and Response Input\n\nhello ｜ goodbye ｜ bye-bye ｜ here ｜ OK ｜ yes ｜ no ｜ thank you ｜ Hello, [Name] ｜ Are you here? ｜ Say here ｜ Say hello ｜ Say goodbye\n\n### Classroom People and Object Input\n\nteacher ｜ friend ｜ classroom ｜ book ｜ bag ｜ chair ｜ table ｜ crayon ｜ pencil ｜ board ｜ door ｜ window ｜ show me your bag ｜ get your book ｜ put it on the table\n\n### Action and Routine Input\n\nlook ｜ listen ｜ sit down ｜ stand up ｜ come here ｜ go back ｜ wait ｜ your turn ｜ point ｜ touch ｜ show me ｜ clean up ｜ put it here ｜ all done\n\n### Teacher Routine Language\n\nHello, [Name]. ｜ Are you here? ｜ Say here. ｜ Look. ｜ Listen. ｜ Sit down. ｜ Stand up. ｜ Come here. ｜ Go back. ｜ Wait. ｜ Your turn. ｜ Point to the book. ｜ Touch the chair. ｜ Show me your bag. ｜ Put it on the table. ｜ Clean up. ｜ Thank you. ｜ Good job.\n\n## 5. Expected PK Response Modes\n\nChildren may respond by looking, waving, saying `Hello`, saying or indicating `Here`, answering with `Yes` or `No` when ready, sitting, standing, coming closer, going back, waiting briefly, taking a turn, pointing, touching, showing objects, naming single classroom objects, getting or returning materials, giving or taking objects, joining clean-up, or using short fixed chunks such as `OK` and `Thank you`.\n\nChildren are not expected to use full self-introduction sentences independently, answer `What’s your name?` in full form, use `It’s a...` independently, answer `Where’s...?`, use `in/on/under` as required output, or complete worksheet-style commands such as circle, tick, or write.\n\n## 6. Theme Songs / Chants\n\n### Core Songs\n\nHello!  \nHello Hello!  \nGood Morning Mr. Rooster  \nBye Bye Goodbye  \nThe More We Get Together  \nFollow Me  \nClean Up Song  \nLine Up!  \nPass the Beanbag  \nMake A Circle\n\n### Use of Songs\n\nSongs should be used to support greeting, roll call, group gathering, circle formation, following teacher, passing, waiting, clean-up, and line-up. Children may sing short familiar words when ready, but movement, gesture, and routine participation remain acceptable baseline responses.\n\n## 7. Resource Opportunities\n\n### Useful EASTIE Spaces\n\nClassroom  \nCircle-time area / mat area  \nBook corner  \nTable activity area  \nToy shelf and classroom storage  \nSmall outdoor grass area attached to PK classroom  \nMusic classroom  \nTheatre / small dramatic play space  \nLego area  \nConstruction / block room\n\n### Useful Materials\n\nName cards  \nPhoto cards  \nAttendance board  \nPuppets  \nClass mascot  \nBooks  \nBags  \nChairs  \nTables  \nCrayons  \nPencils  \nBoard / mini board  \nDoor and window visual cards  \nBaskets  \nTrays  \nFloor spots  \nWalking rope or rings  \nFriend cards  \nTurn-taking object  \nBeanbags  \nClean-up visual cards\n\n### Enhanced Resource Opportunities\n\nA roll-call board, name-card basket, and classroom-object stations can support short response chunks. Classroom object labels can be used visually but should not become reading tasks. Floor spots, walking ropes, and visual cards can support go back, wait, your turn, line-up, and clean-up routines.\n\n## 8. Boundary Notes\n\nThis unit should not become a formal English lesson or self-introduction lesson. `I’m [Name]` may be used as optional challenge only.\n\nThis unit should not require all PK children to answer full verbal questions. Short fixed chunks and single-word attempts are acceptable readiness-based outputs.\n\nThis unit should not use written tasks such as circle, tick, or write as part of the developmental non-language course.\n\nThis unit should not over-focus on colours, numbers, or position language. These may appear naturally but are not the main target of Unit 1.\n\n## 9. Unit-Level Observation Focus\n\nTeachers observe whether children respond to greeting and roll-call routines, use or attempt short fixed response chunks, follow classroom instructions with increasing independence, recognise and handle classroom objects, participate in turn-taking and waiting, join clean-up and line-up routines, and show growing comfort as members of the classroom group.\n\nObservation should focus on classroom participation, routine stability, response confidence, object handling, peer awareness, and readiness-based short output rather than full sentence mastery.\n",
  "zh": "# PK 单元 1：你好与我的班级 — 通用信息\n\n## 单元主题概览\n\nPK 单元 1：你好与我的班级帮助儿童从基础的课堂安顿过渡到更稳定的课堂参与。儿童重新接触问候、师幼互动、同伴意识、课堂物品、点名、轮流、物品处理和收拾常规，使用更广泛的词汇并做出更有意识的反应。\n\n和 PG 相比，PK 儿童在准备好的时候可以使用简短固定句块，例如 “你好”“在这里”“好的”“是”“不是”和“谢谢”，以及单个课堂物品词。但本单元仍是非语言发展单元。不要求儿童产出完整的自我介绍句、回答完整问题，或完成桌面 Starter 风格的任务。\n\n重点是把课堂生活作为社交参与、自我照护、感官探索、故事与木偶互动、假装游戏、创意表达、音乐、建构和 PSED／安全常规的背景。\n\n## 单元语言\n\n### PK 核心词汇\n\nhello ｜ bye-bye ｜ goodbye ｜ teacher ｜ friend ｜ classroom ｜ book ｜ bag ｜ chair ｜ table ｜ crayon ｜ pencil ｜ board ｜ window ｜ door ｜ look ｜ listen ｜ sit down ｜ stand up ｜ come here ｜ go back ｜ wait ｜ your turn ｜ point ｜ touch ｜ show me ｜ clean up ｜ here ｜ OK ｜ yes ｜ no ｜ thank you\n\n### 实用教师句块\n\nHello, [Name]. ｜ Say hello. ｜ Say goodbye. ｜ Look. ｜ Listen. ｜ Sit down. ｜ Stand up. ｜ Come here. ｜ Go back. ｜ Wait. ｜ It's your turn. ｜ Point to the book. ｜ Touch the chair. ｜ Show me your bag. ｜ Get your book. ｜ Put it on the table. ｜ Put the crayon here. ｜ Clean up. ｜ [Name], are you here? ｜ Are you here? Yes or no? ｜ Say here. ｜ I'm [Name]. ｜ Good job. ｜ Thank you.\n\n## 周次子主题\n\n### 第一周：你好、点名和老师\n\n儿童强化问候、点名、教师反应和课堂入座常规。重点是回应姓名、说或示意 “在这里”、问候教师，以及在教师和集体空间之间移动。\n\n### 第二周：我的课堂物品\n\n儿童通过书、包、椅子、桌子、蜡笔、铅笔、板、门和窗重新接触并扩展课堂物品语言。重点是拿取、展示、放置和归还材料。\n\n### 第三周：我的朋友和我的轮次\n\n儿童练习同伴意识、轮流、等待、传递、给予、拿取，以及在准备好时使用支持的简短句块，例如 “我的轮次”“你的轮次”“谢谢”和“好的”。\n\n### 第四周：课堂常规与收拾\n\n儿童把问候、点名、课堂物品、轮流和收拾整合为更完整的课堂常规。重点是稳定参与和逐步增强的独立性。\n\n## 教师输入库\n\n### 问候与回应输入\n\nhello ｜ goodbye ｜ bye-bye ｜ here ｜ OK ｜ yes ｜ no ｜ thank you ｜ Hello, [Name] ｜ Are you here? ｜ Say here ｜ Say hello ｜ Say goodbye\n\n### 班级人物与物品输入\n\nteacher ｜ friend ｜ classroom ｜ book ｜ bag ｜ chair ｜ table ｜ crayon ｜ pencil ｜ board ｜ door ｜ window ｜ show me your bag ｜ get your book ｜ put it on the table\n\n### 动作与常规输入\n\nlook ｜ listen ｜ sit down ｜ stand up ｜ come here ｜ go back ｜ wait ｜ your turn ｜ point ｜ touch ｜ show me ｜ clean up ｜ put it here ｜ all done\n\n### 教师常规语言\n\nHello, [Name]. ｜ Are you here? ｜ Say here. ｜ Look. ｜ Listen. ｜ Sit down. ｜ Stand up. ｜ Come here. ｜ Go back. ｜ Wait. ｜ Your turn. ｜ Point to the book. ｜ Touch the chair. ｜ Show me your bag. ｜ Put it on the table. ｜ Clean up. ｜ Thank you. ｜ Good job.\n\n## 预期 PK 反应方式\n\n儿童可能通过以下方式反应：看、挥手、说“你好”、说或示意“在这里”、在准备好时用“是”或“不是”回应、坐下、站起、走近、走回、短暂等待、加入一次轮流、指、触摸、展示物品、命名单个课堂物品、拿取或归还材料、给予或拿取物品、加入收拾，或在准备好时使用简短固定句块，例如“好的”和“谢谢”。\n\n不要求儿童独立使用完整自我介绍句、完整回答\"What's your name?\"、独立使用\"It's a...\"、回答\"Where's...?\"、把\"in/on/under\"作为必需输出，或完成圈、勾、写等练习册风格的指令。\n\n## 单元核心歌曲\n\n### 核心歌曲\n\nHello!  \nHello Hello!  \nGood Morning Mr. Rooster  \nBye Bye Goodbye  \nThe More We Get Together  \nFollow Me  \nClean Up Song  \nLine Up!  \nPass the Beanbag  \nMake A Circle\n\n### 歌曲的使用\n\n歌曲应当用于支持问候、点名、集体聚集、围圈、跟随教师、传递、等待、收拾和排队。儿童在准备好时可以唱熟悉的简短词，但动作、手势和常规参与仍是可接受的基线反应。\n\n## 资源机会\n\n### 可用 EASTIE 空间\n\nClassroom  \nCircle-time area / mat area  \nBook corner  \nTable activity area  \nToy shelf and classroom storage  \nSmall outdoor grass area attached to PK classroom  \nMusic classroom  \nTheatre / small dramatic play space  \nLego area  \nConstruction / block room\n\n### 可用材料\n\nName cards  \nPhoto cards  \nAttendance board  \nPuppets  \nClass mascot  \nBooks  \nBags  \nChairs  \nTables  \nCrayons  \nPencils  \nBoard / mini board  \nDoor and window visual cards  \nBaskets  \nTrays  \nFloor spots  \nWalking rope or rings  \nFriend cards  \nTurn-taking object  \nBeanbags  \nClean-up visual cards\n\n### 拓展资源机会\n\n点名板、姓名卡篮和课堂物品站可以支持简短反应句块。课堂物品标签可以视觉使用，但不应变成阅读任务。地面标记、行进绳和视觉卡片可以支持“走回去”“等待”“你的轮次”“排队”和“收拾”等常规。\n\n## 边界说明\n\n本单元不应变成正式英语课或自我介绍课。\"I'm [Name]\" 仅可作为可选挑战使用。\n\n本单元不应要求所有 PK 儿童完整口头回答问题。简短固定句块和单词尝试是可接受的准备度导向的输出。\n\n本单元不应使用圈、勾、写等书写任务作为非语言发展课程的一部分。\n\n本单元不应过度聚焦于颜色、数字或方位语言。这些可以自然出现，但不是单元 1 的主要目标。\n\n## 单元观察重点\n\n教师观察儿童是否回应问候和点名常规、使用或尝试简短固定反应句块、越来越独立地跟随课堂指令、识别和处理课堂物品、参与轮流和等待、加入收拾和排队常规，以及在课堂集体中显示出越来越舒适的归属感。\n\n观察应聚焦于课堂参与、常规稳定性、回应信心、物品处理、同伴意识和准备度导向的简短输出，而非完整句子掌握。\n"
};

